<article class="prose">
  <h1>About Huddle Board</h1>
  <p>Huddle Board started on a real sideline, coaching 8U flag football. The coaches knew
    football; the hard part was explaining it to six seven-year-olds in the twenty
    seconds between plays. So the tool does not simplify the football. It simplifies the
    explaining.</p>

  <h2>What it is</h2>
  <p>One web page. It opens on an 11-inch tablet, works with no network at all, and
    installs to the home screen so it runs full screen. A coach builds a deck for the
    game, picks a play, and turns the tablet around.</p>
  <p>Everything a coach sets up — the deck, renamed plays, saved weeks, drawn plays —
    stays on that tablet. There is no account and nothing is sent anywhere.</p>

  <h2>How the rules were made</h2>
  <p>Every rule in the app exists because breaking it made the tool worse for actual
    eight-year-olds. Letters on the field were tried at a real practice and did not land.
    Left and right were tried and did not hold. A play checker rejects geometry a kid
    cannot run, before it ever reaches a tablet.</p>

  <h2>Free, and staying that way</h2>
  <p>Huddle Board is MIT licensed. Copy it, fork it, put your own plays in it, ship it to
    your league. The source and its history are on
    <a href="https://github.com/crawfordjustin/huddle-board">GitHub</a>.</p>

  <p><a class="go big" href="/app/">Open Huddle Board</a></p>
</article>
